		<title type="html"><![CDATA[TYT MD-9600 V5 2571 Dead USB Port]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=4175&amp;p=31759#p31759"><![CDATA[
Hello all,<br><br>I was hoping for some advice on my MD-9600. I bought it at an estate sale (silent key) for a reasonable price. It's already programmed for the area that I live in. I want to use the CPS software to make some changes.  Unfortunately, it seems like the USB port is dead.<br><br>I've used a standard USB cable, a TYT-PROG-9600 cable, and another TYT COM-based cable. Using the first two, the host computer (Windows or Mac) does not respond. There is no indication that a USB device has been plugged in. I've used a combination of GUI and terminal apps to query the USB controllers. The COM-based cable creates a COM port in Windows Device Manager, but I now know it is the wrong cable type.<br><br>I've opened the case a couple of times. The board is V5. Recently, I opened it to see if I could find any damage related to the USB port. I work in IT at a school, and it's not unusual for a USB port to become damaged if stressed. I noticed a capacitor (470) on traces leading to the antenna connection. It had a white substance around the cap, but after scrapping it away, it didn't appear the capacitor was blown. There is white thermal paste in other parts of the radio. Maybe some migrated over there.<br><br>I didn't take the board entirely out, as I found the antenna connector is soldered to the board and would have to be desoldered to remove it. The USB port is on the bottom of the board.<br><br>The radio works fine otherwise.<br><br>Has anyone here had experience with a failed USB port on the MD-9600? Is there a way to disable it via software?<br><br>Thanks for your help.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=6338">KC1UUF</a> — Sun Jun 23, 2024 6:03 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
